1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,764
Let A be a two by two matrix, and v a vector in the plane.

2
00:00:04,764 --> 00:00:07,485
Usually A v points somewhere new.

3
00:00:07,485 --> 00:00:11,632
But sometimes A v lands on the same line as v.

4
00:00:11,632 --> 00:00:15,132
Then A v is just a scalar multiple of v.

5
00:00:15,132 --> 00:00:18,882
We write this as A v equals lambda v.

6
00:00:18,882 --> 00:00:23,941
The vector v, which must not be zero, is called an eigenvector.

7
00:00:23,941 --> 00:00:26,691
The number lambda is its eigenvalue.

8
00:00:26,691 --> 00:00:30,779
If lambda is bigger than one, the vector stretches.

9
00:00:30,779 --> 00:00:33,955
Between zero and one, it shrinks.

10
00:00:33,955 --> 00:00:36,602
If lambda is negative, it flips.

11
00:00:36,602 --> 00:00:41,088
Our goal: find all such pairs, lambda and v.

